tgloggerd web application
=========================

The React application behind the tgloggerd web UI. Pages are rendered
to HTML on the server and then taken over by the browser; the design
is in ../../docs/design/web-ssr.md (server rendering and the render
protocol) and ../../docs/design/web-live-client.md (live updates). The
visual design comes from ../mockup.

  - The Go service (webapi/) authenticates, applies access rules, loads
    each page's data and serves the API under /api/.
  - The render service in server/ is a small Node process that turns a
    page's route and data into HTML for the Go service, over a Unix
    socket. It has no database access, cookies or secrets.
  - The browser bundle (src/entry-client.tsx) hydrates that HTML and
    navigates without reloads, fetching page data from /api/page/.

Everything runs through Docker; nothing but Docker is needed on the
host:

    ./dev npm ci
    ./dev npm run typecheck
    ./dev npm test
    ./dev npm run build
    ./dev npm run check:api

`npm run build` (scripts/build.ts) writes the client bundle to
dist/client (content-hashed files and the manifest the Go service
reads) and the render entry to dist/server, both with one build id,
and records that id last in dist/client/build.json, where the Go
service reads it. The id is a hash of the inputs (package.json, the
lock file, tsconfig.json, vite.config.ts and src/), so the same tree
gives the same id; TGD_BUILD (passed through by ./dev) sets another.

The API's types are generated from the Go service's OpenAPI
description, ../../webapi/openapi.yaml, into src/api/generated/ by
`npm run gen:api`, and committed. After changing the description, run
it and commit the result with the change; `npm run check:api` fails
while the committed file differs from what the description gives.
